<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Orchomenus (Boeotia)</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Orchomenus</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ancient Boeotian city, heir of Mycenaean Orchomenos, which rivalled Thebes for leadership of Boeotia until the Thebans destroyed it in 364 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the collapse of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mycenaean-kingdom-of-orchomenos">Mycenaean Kingdom of Orchomenos</a> c. 1200 BCE, the town survived beside the refilled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-copais">Lake Copais</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> it became a Boeotian polis and the main rival of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thebes">Thebes</a>, sometimes leading western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>. Its cult of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charites">Graces</a>, the Charites, was among the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Orchomenus was a member of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League</a> and an oligarchy of landowners. It sided with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a> against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thebes">Thebes</a> in the early 4th century BCE and fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-coronea-394-bc">Coronea</a> in 394 BCE. Its festival of the Charitesia attracted poets and musicians, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a> wrote an ode for an Orchomenian victor.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thebes">Thebes</a> destroyed Orchomenus in 364 BCE, killing its men. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II</a> rebuilt it after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaeronea-338-bce">Chaeronea</a> in 338 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates</a>'s army nearby in 85 BCE, and the town, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from 146 BCE, continued on a smaller scale.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/orchomenus-boeotia.jpg" alt="Orchomenus (Boeotia)" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: George E. Koronaios · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_derelict_state_of_the_tower_on_the_top_of_the_Acropolis_of_Orchomenus_on_September_30,_2020.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Orchomenus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; cult of the Charites</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Boeotian Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Greece</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Orchomenos</dd></dl></aside></div>

